\chapter{Too many zeros: zero-inflated and hurdle models}

\section{Two kinds of zero}

Ask people how many cigarettes they smoked yesterday. You get lots of zeros, but
they are not all the same kind of zero:

\begin{itemize}
  \item \textbf{Structural zeros} come from people who do not smoke at all. For
        them the count \emph{cannot} be anything but zero.
  \item \textbf{Sampling zeros} come from smokers who happened not to smoke
        yesterday. For them zero is just a low draw from a count process.
\end{itemize}

A Poisson or negative binomial has only one mechanism, so it must explain both
kinds with the same parameter. When the structural group is large, it cannot, and
you get more zeros than any single count distribution can produce.

\begin{plainenglish}[The test question]
``Is there a group in my data for whom the event is impossible, not merely
unlikely?'' If yes, you probably want zero-inflation. If no --- everyone could in
principle have a non-zero count --- a negative binomial usually handles the zeros
by itself.
\end{plainenglish}

\section{The zero-inflated model}

A zero-inflated model is a \emph{mixture} of two processes:
\[
\Pr(Y_i = 0) = \pi_i + (1 - \pi_i)\,f(0; \mu_i)
\]
\[
\Pr(Y_i = k) = (1 - \pi_i)\,f(k; \mu_i), \qquad k = 1, 2, 3, \dots
\]
where $\pi_i$ is the probability of being in the ``always zero'' group (modelled
with a logistic regression) and $f$ is a Poisson or negative binomial (modelled
with a log link).

\begin{center}
\begin{tikzpicture}[font=\sffamily\scriptsize, node distance=4mm,
  b/.style={rectangle, rounded corners=2pt, draw=rule1, fill=white, align=center,
            inner sep=4pt, text width=32mm},
  ar/.style={-{Stealth[length=4pt]}, draw=rule1, thick}]
\node[b, fill=primarylt, draw=primary, text width=22mm] (s) {Each observation};
\node[b, above right=2mm and 12mm of s, fill=redlt, draw=redbox] (z)
  {\textbf{Structural zero}\\prob.\ $\pi_i$ (logistic)\\count is always 0};
\node[b, below right=2mm and 12mm of s, fill=greenlt, draw=greenbox] (c)
  {\textbf{Count process}\\prob.\ $1-\pi_i$ (log link)\\Poisson or NB, may give 0};
\node[b, fill=amberlt, draw=amber, text width=24mm]
  (o) at ($(z)!0.5!(c) + (3.4cm,0)$) {\textbf{Observed zero}\\could come from\\either route};
\draw[ar] (s) -- (z); \draw[ar] (s) -- (c);
\draw[ar] (z) -- (o); \draw[ar] (c) -- (o);
\end{tikzpicture}
\end{center}

\section{A worked example}

\fn{bioChemists} records the number of articles published by 915 PhD biochemistry
students in their last three years of study.

<<bio-data>>=
data(bioChemists, package = "pscl")
str(bioChemists, give.attr = FALSE)
table(bioChemists$art)
round(100 * mean(bioChemists$art == 0), 1)     # percent zeros
@

\Sexpr{round(100 * mean(bioChemists$art == 0), 1)}\% published nothing. Some of
those were surely trying and unlucky; others were probably never on a publication
track at all. That is exactly the two-group structure zero-inflation describes.

<<bio-baseline>>=
mp  <- glm(art ~ fem + mar + kid5 + phd + ment, family = poisson, data = bioChemists)
mnb <- MASS::glm.nb(art ~ fem + mar + kid5 + phd + ment, data = bioChemists)

c(observed_zeros  = sum(bioChemists$art == 0),
  poisson_expects = round(sum(dpois(0, fitted(mp))), 1),
  negbin_expects  = round(sum(dnbinom(0, mu = fitted(mnb), size = mnb$theta)), 1))
@

The Poisson expects about \Sexpr{round(sum(dpois(0, fitted(mp))))} zeros against
\Sexpr{sum(bioChemists$art == 0)} observed --- a serious shortfall. The negative
binomial does much better on its own, which is already a useful clue.

\subsection{Fitting a zero-inflated model}

The formula has two parts separated by \fn{|}: the count model on the left, the
zero-inflation model on the right.

<<zip-fit>>=
library(pscl)
zip <- zeroinfl(art ~ fem + mar + kid5 + phd + ment | fem + mar + kid5 + phd + ment,
                data = bioChemists, dist = "poisson")
summary(zip)
@

\begin{keyidea}[Reading a two-part printout]
\textbf{Count model coefficients} are on the log scale and apply to everyone
\emph{not} in the structural-zero group. Exponentiate them for rate ratios. \\
\textbf{Zero-inflation model coefficients} are on the logit scale and predict
membership of the ``always zero'' group. A \emph{positive} coefficient means
\emph{more likely to be a structural zero}, i.e.\ less likely to publish at all.
\end{keyidea}

<<zip-interp>>=
round(exp(coef(zip, model = "count")), 3)     # rate ratios among "potential publishers"
round(exp(coef(zip, model = "zero")), 3)      # odds ratios for being a structural zero
@

For example, \fn{ment} (articles by the student's mentor) has a negative
zero-inflation coefficient, so students with productive mentors are less likely to
fall in the never-publishing group --- which is exactly what you would expect.

\subsection{Zero-inflated negative binomial}

You can zero-inflate a negative binomial too, which handles excess zeros
\emph{and} extra variability at once.

<<zinb-fit>>=
zinb <- zeroinfl(art ~ fem + mar + kid5 + phd + ment | fem + mar + kid5 + phd + ment,
                 data = bioChemists, dist = "negbin")
round(c(AIC_poisson = AIC(mp), AIC_negbin = AIC(mnb),
        AIC_zip = AIC(zip), AIC_zinb = AIC(zinb)), 1)
@

\begin{plainenglish}[What that AIC table says]
The plain Poisson is much the worst. Zero-inflating it helps a lot. But the plain
negative binomial does nearly as well as the ZIP with far fewer parameters, and the
ZINB is barely better than the NB. In other words: the ``excess zeros'' here were
mostly just overdispersion in disguise.
\end{plainenglish}

\section{Hurdle models}

A hurdle model splits the problem differently:

\begin{enumerate}
  \item \textbf{Did you clear the hurdle?} A binary model for zero versus non-zero.
  \item \textbf{Given that you did, how far?} A \emph{zero-truncated} count model
        for the positive values.
\end{enumerate}

The crucial difference: in a hurdle model \emph{all} zeros come from the first
part. In a zero-inflated model, zeros can come from either part.

<<hurdle-fit>>=
hnb <- hurdle(art ~ fem + mar + kid5 + phd + ment | fem + mar + kid5 + phd + ment,
              data = bioChemists, dist = "negbin")
summary(hnb)
@

\begin{warningbox}[The sign convention flips]
In \fn{zeroinfl}, the binary part models the probability of being a \emph{zero}. In
\fn{hurdle}, it models the probability of being \emph{positive}. So the same
predictor gets opposite signs in the two printouts. Check which function produced a
table before interpreting its signs.
\end{warningbox}

\begin{center}
\small\sffamily
\begin{tabular}{@{}p{0.22\textwidth}p{0.37\textwidth}p{0.37\textwidth}@{}}
\toprule
 & \textbf{Zero-inflated} & \textbf{Hurdle} \\
\midrule
Where zeros come from & two sources: structural and sampling & one source only \\
Binary part models    & $\Pr(\text{structural zero})$ & $\Pr(\text{count} > 0)$ \\
Count part            & standard Poisson/NB           & zero-\emph{truncated} Poisson/NB \\
Natural story         & a latent subgroup that can never have the event & a two-stage decision: first whether, then how much \\
Can handle too \emph{few} zeros & no & yes \\
Typical use           & ``never-smokers'' mixed in with smokers & doctor visits: first you decide to go, then how often \\
\bottomrule
\end{tabular}
\end{center}

\begin{rulebox}
If you can name the latent group that structurally cannot have the event, use
zero-inflation. If the process is genuinely two decisions in sequence, use a
hurdle. If neither story is convincing, you probably just have overdispersion ---
use a negative binomial.
\end{rulebox}

\section{Which model reproduces the data best?}

Forget the AIC table for a moment and just count what each model predicts.

<<zero-compare>>=
zeros_expected <- c(
  observed = sum(bioChemists$art == 0),
  poisson  = sum(dpois(0, fitted(mp))),
  negbin   = sum(dnbinom(0, mu = fitted(mnb), size = mnb$theta)),
  zip      = sum(predict(zip,  type = "prob")[, 1]),
  zinb     = sum(predict(zinb, type = "prob")[, 1]),
  hurdle   = sum(predict(hnb,  type = "prob")[, 1])
)
round(zeros_expected, 1)
@

Zero-inflated and hurdle models reproduce the zero count almost exactly --- they
have a parameter dedicated to it, so that is guaranteed and not evidence of
anything. The informative comparison is over the \emph{whole} distribution.

<<zero-plot, fig.width=9.4, fig.height=4.2, out.width="\\textwidth", fig.cap="Left: expected frequency of each count under four models. Right: rootograms for the Poisson and the negative binomial. The Poisson misses the zeros badly; the negative binomial does not.">>=
par(mfrow = c(1, 2)); par_book()
maxk <- 10
obs  <- as.numeric(table(factor(pmin(bioChemists$art, maxk), levels = 0:maxk)))
e_p  <- sapply(0:maxk, function(k) sum(dpois(k, fitted(mp))))
e_nb <- sapply(0:maxk, function(k) sum(dnbinom(k, mu = fitted(mnb), size = mnb$theta)))
e_zi <- colSums(predict(zip,  type = "prob")[, 1:(maxk + 1)])
e_hu <- colSums(predict(hnb,  type = "prob")[, 1:(maxk + 1)])

plot(0:maxk, obs, type = "h", lwd = 6, col = adjustcolor(PAL["grey"], 0.5),
     xlab = "articles published", ylab = "frequency",
     main = "Observed vs expected frequencies")
grid_book()
lines(0:maxk, e_p,  lwd = 2.5, col = PAL["red"])
lines(0:maxk, e_nb, lwd = 2.5, col = PAL["green"])
lines(0:maxk, e_zi, lwd = 2.5, lty = 2, col = PAL["primary"])
lines(0:maxk, e_hu, lwd = 2.5, lty = 3, col = PAL["accent"])
legend("topright", bty = "n", cex = 0.75, lwd = c(6, 2.5, 2.5, 2.5, 2.5),
       lty = c(1, 1, 1, 2, 3),
       col = c(adjustcolor(PAL["grey"], 0.5), PAL["red"], PAL["green"],
               PAL["primary"], PAL["accent"]),
       legend = c("observed", "Poisson", "neg. binomial", "ZIP", "hurdle NB"))

plot(c(-0.5, maxk + 0.5), c(-6, max(sqrt(e_p)) * 1.1), type = "n",
     xlab = "articles published", ylab = expression(sqrt("frequency")),
     main = "Rootograms: Poisson (red) vs NB (green)")
grid_book()
rect(0:maxk - 0.42, sqrt(e_p) - sqrt(obs), 0:maxk - 0.02, sqrt(e_p),
     col = adjustcolor(PAL["red"], 0.35), border = "white")
rect(0:maxk + 0.02, sqrt(e_nb) - sqrt(obs), 0:maxk + 0.42, sqrt(e_nb),
     col = adjustcolor(PAL["green"], 0.35), border = "white")
abline(h = 0, lwd = 2, col = PAL["grey"])
lines(0:maxk, sqrt(e_p),  lwd = 2, col = PAL["red"])
lines(0:maxk, sqrt(e_nb), lwd = 2, col = PAL["green"])
@

\section{The Vuong test, and why to be careful with it}

The Vuong test compares two \emph{non-nested} models by looking at whether one
assigns higher likelihood to the individual observations than the other.

<<vuong, message=TRUE>>=
vuong(zip, mp)      # zero-inflated Poisson vs plain Poisson
@

\begin{warningbox}[Do not use Vuong for zero-inflation versus its own base model]
A zero-inflated Poisson \emph{contains} the Poisson as the special case $\pi = 0$,
so the two are nested, not non-nested --- and $\pi = 0$ sits on the boundary of the
parameter space. Vuong's theory assumes neither. Simulation studies find the test
badly behaved for exactly this comparison, and it tends to favour the
zero-inflated model too often. Use AIC/BIC and a rootogram instead. Vuong is
legitimate for genuinely non-nested comparisons, such as ZIP versus hurdle-NB.
\end{warningbox}

<<vuong-ok, message=TRUE>>=
vuong(zinb, hnb)    # ZINB vs hurdle-NB: genuinely non-nested, so this is fair
@

\section{A case with real zero-inflation}

\fn{bioChemists} turned out not to need zero-inflation. Here is a dataset that
does: boating trips taken in a year, where most respondents took none.

<<rec-data>>=
data(RecreationDemand, package = "AER")
round(100 * mean(RecreationDemand$trips == 0), 1)       # percent zeros
mp_r  <- glm(trips ~ quality + ski + income + userfee + costC + costS + costH,
             family = poisson, data = RecreationDemand)
mnb_r <- MASS::glm.nb(trips ~ quality + ski + income + userfee + costC + costS + costH,
                      data = RecreationDemand)
zinb_r <- zeroinfl(trips ~ quality + ski + income + userfee + costC + costS + costH |
                   quality + income, data = RecreationDemand, dist = "negbin")
hnb_r  <- hurdle(trips ~ quality + ski + income + userfee + costC + costS + costH |
                 quality + income, data = RecreationDemand, dist = "negbin")

round(c(observed = sum(RecreationDemand$trips == 0),
        poisson  = sum(dpois(0, fitted(mp_r))),
        negbin   = sum(dnbinom(0, mu = fitted(mnb_r), size = mnb_r$theta)),
        zinb     = sum(predict(zinb_r, type = "prob")[, 1]),
        hurdle   = sum(predict(hnb_r,  type = "prob")[, 1])), 1)
round(c(AIC_pois = AIC(mp_r), AIC_nb = AIC(mnb_r),
        AIC_zinb = AIC(zinb_r), AIC_hurdle = AIC(hnb_r)), 1)
@

Here the story is different: the zero-inflated and hurdle models are clearly better
than the negative binomial on AIC, and only they get the zero count right. This is
what a genuine need for zero-inflation looks like.

\begin{keyidea}[The decision procedure]
\begin{enumerate}
  \item Fit Poisson. Count observed zeros against predicted zeros.
  \item Fit negative binomial. Count again. Usually the shortfall is gone.
  \item If a large shortfall \emph{remains}, and you can tell a story about a
        structural-zero group, fit ZI or hurdle models.
  \item Compare with AIC/BIC and, above all, with a rootogram.
\end{enumerate}
\end{keyidea}

\begin{warningbox}[Zero-inflation is over-used]
Many published zero-inflated models were fitted because someone saw a lot of zeros
and stopped there. Lots of zeros is completely normal for a count with a small
mean --- a Poisson with mean 0.5 produces 61\% zeros all by itself. The question is
never ``are there many zeros?'' but ``are there more zeros \emph{than my model
predicts}?''
\end{warningbox}

<<zeros-normal>>=
round(sapply(c(0.5, 1, 2, 5), function(m) dpois(0, m)), 3)   # % zeros by Poisson mean
@
